b/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183921 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183922 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183922 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183922 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183923 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183923 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183923 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184238 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function